Hi Jun,

We wanted to further elaborate on JR8 and clarify our strategy for reducing
metadata read fan-out, as your question highlighted a key nuance.

You're correct that the fan-out can be a problem with the default
configuration. For example, in a cluster with 50 brokers and 5000 data
partitions, a broker will on average lead 100 partitions and follow 200
partitions. If these are randomly distributed across the 50 default
metadata partitions, the broker is forced to subscribe to all 50 to
guarantee it receives its updates. Our solution has two components to
address this.

First, our current implementation splits metadata for efficient processing.
For each combined object uploaded to S3, we publish two types of messages:
1. A single, larger metadata message for the entire combined log segment.
2. Multiple very small, individual metadata messages, one for each data
partition contained within that combined segment.
This ensures that a follower broker only needs to process the smaller
message specific to the data partition it is replicating, not the large
message for the entire batch.

However, this still leaves the problem of waking up every broker for every
small message. To solve that, there are two options:
(a) Deterministic Mapping (described in previous reply): We could create a
mapping where the 200 partitions followed by Broker A are explicitly mapped
to metadata partitions 1 and 2. This would ensure an update only wakes up
the relevant brokers, but the mapping is rigid and could become unbalanced
over time.
(b) Increase Metadata Partition Count (mentioned briefly in previous
reply): Instead of the default 50, we would increase the number of metadata
partitions to be on par with the number of data partitions=E2=80=94for exam=
ple,
5000 metadata partitions for 5000 data partitions. In this scenario, a
broker following 200 data partitions simply subscribes to those 200
specific metadata partitions. When a metadata message is published, it is
sent to its dedicated metadata partition and will only be consumed by the
handful of brokers subscribed to it.

We believe approach (b) is the more scalable and resilient solution.
Pending further discussion here, we will add these details and the chosen
strategy to the KIP.

Thanks,
Tom & Henry

