--0000000000001a5c5306404222c1
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi, Tom and Henry,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1: I am still not very clear on how this works.
"Comprehensive Approach: A safe method where the new leader re-uploads all
log segments newer than the topic's local.retention.ms configuration."
Hmm, does that mean that the leader is not available until the re-uploads
complete? If so, this will significantly reduce the availability.

"Optimized Approach: The new leader identifies and re-uploads only the log
segments that have not yet been tiered to long-term storage. Followers
needing older data can still fetch it directly from the long-term tiered
storage, reducing the amount of data that needs to be immediately
republished."
In this approach, the follower will be blocked from joining ISR if it tries
to read a metadata pointing to a S3E1Z that's not currently available.

"Followers are designed to handle this situation gracefully. When a
follower consumes metadata, it will first try to fetch data from the
location specified. If it encounters the old metadata from the failed
leader, the fetch will fail. The follower will then ignore this stale
metadata and proceed to the next entry, which will be the valid metadata
from the new leader."
How does the follower know whether a metadata is stale and can be ignored?
How does the follower handle duplicated or out of order metadata?

It would be useful to improve the description in the wiki with more details
on leader failover, bootstrapping a new replica, etc.

JR2.
"Correct, grouping partitions into smaller objects increases the number of
write requests, which slightly raises the writer-side request cost. This is
a deliberate tradeoff. In scenarios with high reader fan-out, the total
cost is dominated by the readers. By accepting a small increase in writer
cost, we can achieve a much larger reduction in reader cost, leading to a
lower overall system cost."

Let's consider the cost of writing 0.3MB worth of data across 100
partitions in one object

write cost =3D 1 put request cost + transfer cost
($0.00113) / (1000 PUT) + ($0.0032 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 1.13 * 10^-6 + 0.96 =
*
10^-6 =3D $2.09 * 10^-6

If we split it into 10 put requests
write cost =3D 10 put request cost + transfer cost
10 * ($0.00113) / (1000 PUT) + ($0.0032 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 1.13 * 10^-5 +
0.96 * 10^-6 =3D $1.23 * 10^-5

This makes the write cost 6 times larger, not a small increase. So, I think
there is still a cost issue in the KIP that needs to be addressed.


JR8:
"For a topic with 30 MB/s ingress, a 3MB batch accumulates in 100ms. Adding
~20ms for S3 Express upload and download results in a total replication
latency of ~120ms, which is acceptable for many logging and analytics use
cases (especially in exchange for cross-AZ cost savings)."

I thought the goal of this KIP is to not increase latency? If logging is
the only use case, you might just go with S3, which has higher latency but
even cheaper cost.

"(a) Deterministic Mapping (described in previous reply): We could create a
mapping where the 200 partitions followed by Broker A are explicitly mapped
to metadata partitions 1 and 2. This would ensure an update only wakes up
the relevant brokers, but the mapping is rigid and could become unbalanced
over time."

Hmm, so if a replica changes from a leader to a follower, does the metadata
of that partition change to a different metadata_partition? Since a replica
receives the leader/follower info asynchronously, how does it know when to
switch to read from a different metadata partition without missing
metadata? How does this work with RF=3D3? Do you double publish the metadat=
a
for each follower? How does this work when the replicas are reassigned to
different brokers?


"(b) Increase Metadata Partition Count (mentioned briefly in previous
reply): Instead of the default 50, we would increase the number of metadata
partitions to be on par with the number of data partitions=E2=80=94for exam=
ple,
5000 metadata partitions for 5000 data partitions. In this scenario, a
broker following 200 data partitions simply subscribes to those 200
specific metadata partitions. When a metadata message is published, it is
sent to its dedicated metadata partition and will only be consumed by the
handful of brokers subscribed to it."

This is not ideal either. We don't want to over-partition an internal topic
to hide a design inefficiency. Many features are utilizing internal topics.
If every feature follows this approach, the number of partitions will be
out of control.

Jun

On Wed, Oct 1, 2025 at 12:50=E2=80=AFPM Thomas Thornton
<tthornton@salesforce.com.invalid> wrote:

> Hi Jun,
>
> We wanted to further elaborate on JR8 and clarify our strategy for reduci=
ng
> metadata read fan-out, as your question highlighted a key nuance.
>
> You're correct that the fan-out can be a problem with the default
> configuration. For example, in a cluster with 50 brokers and 5000 data
> partitions, a broker will on average lead 100 partitions and follow 200
> partitions. If these are randomly distributed across the 50 default
> metadata partitions, the broker is forced to subscribe to all 50 to
> guarantee it receives its updates. Our solution has two components to
> address this.
>
> First, our current implementation splits metadata for efficient processin=
g.
> For each combined object uploaded to S3, we publish two types of messages=
:
> 1. A single, larger metadata message for the entire combined log segment.
> 2. Multiple very small, individual metadata messages, one for each data
> partition contained within that combined segment.
> This ensures that a follower broker only needs to process the smaller
> message specific to the data partition it is replicating, not the large
> message for the entire batch.
>
> However, this still leaves the problem of waking up every broker for ever=
y
> small message. To solve that, there are two options:
> (a) Deterministic Mapping (described in previous reply): We could create =
a
> mapping where the 200 partitions followed by Broker A are explicitly mapp=
ed
> to metadata partitions 1 and 2. This would ensure an update only wakes up
> the relevant brokers, but the mapping is rigid and could become unbalance=
d
> over time.
> (b) Increase Metadata Partition Count (mentioned briefly in previous
> reply): Instead of the default 50, we would increase the number of metada=
ta
> partitions to be on par with the number of data partitions=E2=80=94for ex=
ample,
> 5000 metadata partitions for 5000 data partitions. In this scenario, a
> broker following 200 data partitions simply subscribes to those 200
> specific metadata partitions. When a metadata message is published, it is
> sent to its dedicated metadata partition and will only be consumed by the
> handful of brokers subscribed to it.
>
> We believe approach (b) is the more scalable and resilient solution.
> Pending further discussion here, we will add these details and the chosen
> strategy to the KIP.
>
> Thanks,
> Tom & Henry
