Hi Jun,

Thanks for your feedback and helping us refine this KIP.

JR1:

The recovery process for S3 Express One Zone during an AZ failure involves
trade-offs. To address this, we will support two storage backends as
user-configurable options:
1. Standard S3: For users who require maximum availability and simpler
recovery. This avoids the S3E1Z AZ failure scenario at the cost of higher
latency.
2. S3 Express One Zone: For users who prioritize lower latency and accept a
longer recovery time during a rare AZ failure.

Since S3 is replicated across three AZs we can treat the data as fully
replicated once the write is complete. The next answers are just in
response to the S3 Express One Zone path.

> Hmm, does that mean that the leader is not available until the re-uploads
> complete? If so, this will significantly reduce the availability.

Yes, this is correct. Failure of the whole AZ has shown to be very rare,
and is a tradeoff users make for lower latency/cost and potentially longer
recovery times. If the whole AZ down situation is something the customer is
really worried about, maybe S3 is a better choice for the customer

> In this approach, the follower will be blocked from joining ISR if it
tries
> to read a metadata pointing to a S3E1Z that's not currently available.

The follower won't be permanently blocked. It will attempt to fetch data
from the inaccessible S3E1Z location, the request will fail (e.g.,
timeout), and the follower will log the error and move to the next metadata
record. It will catch up and can join the ISR once it processes the new
metadata from the new leader pointing to the accessible data.

> How does the follower know whether a metadata is stale and can be ignored=
?

The follower knows the metadata is not valid when the referencing data is
not accessible in the object storage.

> How does the follower handle duplicated or out of order metadata?

Kafka guarantees metadata is processed in offset order. After a failover,
the new leader publishes new metadata at a later offset. The follower will
first try the old (stale) record, fail, and move on until it reaches the
new, valid record from the new leader. This sequential processing ensures
it always converges on the correct state.

> It would be useful to improve the description in the wiki with more
details
> on leader failover, bootstrapping a new replica, etc.

Agreed, we will update the KIP once we have consensus on these availability
questions in this discussion.

JR2:
> Let's consider the cost of writing 0.3MB worth of data across 100
> partitions in one object
> write cost =3D 1 put request cost + transfer cost
($0.00113) / (1000 PUT) + ($0.0032 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 1.13 * 10^-6 + > 0.9=
6 *
> 10^-6 =3D $2.09 * 10^-6
> If we split it into 10 put requests
> write cost =3D 10 put request cost + transfer cost
> 10 * ($0.00113) / (1000 PUT) + ($0.0032 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 1.13 * 10^-5 =
+
> 0.96 * 10^-6 =3D $1.23 * 10^-5
> This makes the write cost 6 times larger, not a small increase. So, I
think
> there is still a cost issue in the KIP that needs to be addressed.

Our alternative approach, detailed in JR8, eliminates the metadata topic
and the associated fan-out problem. This removes the need for object
splitting, allowing for larger, more cost-effective writes and resolving
this cost issue.

JR8:

Given the concern on our previous proposals on solving metadata read fanout
issues, we propose an alternative way of solving this problem. The new
direction is to eliminate the metadata topic and use the standard data
replication flow for metadata transfer. This approach is more robust:
1. No Fan-Out: Metadata is sent point-to-point directly to followers.
2. Resilience: The design is inherently robust against replica
reassignments and leader changes.
3. Efficiency: It avoids over-partitioning and complex mapping logic.

We originally considered this approach, but didn=E2=80=99t choose it based =
on the
