Hi, Tom,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1.
"Yes, this is correct. Failure of the whole AZ has shown to be very rare,
and is a tradeoff users make for lower latency/cost and potentially longer
recovery times. If the whole AZ down situation is something the customer is
really worried about, maybe S3 is a better choice for the customer"

This is not ideal. A primary reason a user chooses replication is to
achieve higher availability. If availability is impacted by a single AZ
failure, it defeats the purpose of replication.

"The follower won't be permanently blocked. It will attempt to fetch data
from the inaccessible S3E1Z location, the request will fail (e.g.,
timeout), and the follower will log the error and move to the next metadata
record. It will catch up and can join the ISR once it processes the new
metadata from the new leader pointing to the accessible data."

For the optimized approach, the wiki says "The new leader identifies and
re-uploads only the log segments that have not yet been tiered to long-term
storage." By long-term storage, do you mean S3 or S3E1Z? If it's S3, how is
this approach different from the comprehensive approach? If it's S3E1Z,
there will be some portion of the log that's only available in a single
S3E1Z. If a follower needs that portion of the data, it will be stuck.

JR2.
"Our alternative approach, detailed in JR8, eliminates the metadata topic
and the associated fan-out problem. This removes the need for object
splitting, allowing for larger, more cost-effective writes and resolving
this cost issue."

Hmm, I don't see how the alternative approach prevents the same S3E1Z
object from being read 100 times.

"The new direction is to eliminate the metadata topic and use the standard
data replication flow for metadata transfer."

So each user partition now has a metadata partition and a separate data
partition? If so, I had a similar comment on KIP-1150. I am concerned that
it essentially doubles the number of partitions, which impacts the number
of open file descriptors and the required IOPS, and so on. It also seems
wasteful to have a separate partition just to store the metadata. It's as
if we are creating an internal topic with an unbounded number of partitions=
.

Jun

