Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

Hi Jun,

Thanks for the discussion on this.

JR1:

We propose a cleaner failover process below. It removes the ephemeral
condition of trying to access a down AZ as a way to determine which
metadata messages should be ignored (could pose issues if the AZ comes back
online). Additionally, it removes the optimization of trying to re-upload
data after leader failure. Instead, it follows an approach more akin to
what standard Kafka does. First, we will walk through how this leader
failover works for standard Kafka, and then we will describe how this works
for our proposal.

Suppose for a standard Kafka topic, the current leader of a partition
broker B1. B1 has received data up to offset 10. One follower B2 has
replicated up to offset 5, and one follower B3 has replicated up to offset
7. Suppose B1 dies, and B2 is elected as the new leader. B2 overwrites the
LEO/high-watermark based on its own LEO, which is 5. B2 will tell its
followers to reset their LEO to 5. So B3, which had an LEO of 7, will
discard 2 messages, and reset to 5 to match B2, the new leader.

Now for KIP-1176, suppose there is a remote WAL enabled topic also with
replication factor 3 (for a fair comparison). The current leader of a
partition is B1. B1 has received data up to offset 10, written data to
S3E1Z up to offset 10, and published metadata up to offset 10. One
follower, B2, has replicated up to offset 5, and follower B3, up to offset
7. Suppose B1 dies (e.g., AZ goes down), and B2 is elected as the new
leader. B2 overwrites the LEO/high-watermark based on its own LEO, which is
5. B2 will tell its followers to reset their LEO to 5. So B3, which had an
LEO of 7, will discard 2 messages and reset 5 to match B2, the new leader.
In addition, B2 will also publish metadata DELETE events for any metadata
entries it receives that are after its LEO (e.g., for messages 6-10), since
they are no longer valid.

In the case that B3 was elected, with the higher end offset of offset 7. It
will perform replica reconciliation and discard its data that is greater
than the high watermark of 5. This prevents any sacrifices in availability,
and is equivalent to standard Kafka.

"This is not ideal. A primary reason a user chooses replication is to
achieve higher availability. If availability is impacted by a single AZ
failure, it defeats the purpose of replication."

We agree with this concern, and believe the above approach addresses it.

"For the optimized approach, the wiki says "The new leader identifies and
re-uploads only the log segments that have not yet been tiered to long-term
storage." By long-term storage, do you mean S3 or S3E1Z? If it's S3, how is
this approach different from the comprehensive approach? If it's S3E1Z,
there will be some portion of the log that's only available in a single
S3E1Z. If a follower needs that portion of the data, it will be stuck."

This portion is simplified and removed in the above approach. We have
updated the wiki to describe this process.

JR2:

"Hmm, I don't see how the alternative approach prevents the same S3E1Z
object from being read 100 times."

Are you referring to data or metadata?

For data, there is a combiner thread on the leader, which combines data
from multiple partitions into one large S3 object. This same object is
accessed by all followers of those partitions. However, they only access a
specific byte range for the partitions they are following. Thus, it doesn't
matter if they are accessing the same object or separate object. They need
to pay the request cost and data transfer either way.

For metadata, by having a corresponding metadata partition for each data
partition, each follower only reads the metadata that it requires.

"So each user partition now has a metadata partition and a separate data
partition? If so, I had a similar comment on KIP-1150. I am concerned that
it essentially doubles the number of partitions, which impacts the number
of open file descriptors and the required IOPS, and so on. It also seems
wasteful to have a separate partition just to store the metadata. It's as
if we are creating an internal topic with an unbounded number of
partitions."

Correct, for each data partition, there is a 1-1 mapping to the metadata
partition. From all solutions we evaluated, this is the only one where we
can both 1. reduce metadata read fanout and 2. avoid significant
architecture change to add a centralized coordinator (e.g., receives all
metadata and writes to a performant datastore, then as followers request
metadata it performs key-value lookups for offsets)

Regarding file descriptors, with modern machines the total number of file
descriptors open can be well beyond one million (that is often set just for
the kafka process alone). Each file descriptor may take a ~300 bytes of
kernel memory. If we assume a broker leads 1000 partitions. And the newly
added metadata partition has around ~300 file descriptors. Additional added
memory is:
partition_count * file_descriptors * bytes_per_descriptor
1000 * 300 * 300 ~90 MB

Additional file descriptors are:
partition_count * file_descriptors_per_partition
1000 * 300 =3D 300000

Total file descriptors would be twice that so 600k.

These values seem well within system ranges. For example looking at file
descriptors system total max on one of our kafka hosts & the max per proces=
s
$ cat /proc/sys/fs/file-max
9223372036854775807
$ cat /proc/22583/limits | grep "Max open files"
Max open files            1000000              1000000              files
$ sudo slabtop
 Active / Total Size (% used)       : 2590276.09K / 2884225.98K (89.8%)

So total file descriptors would remain below the initial lower bound of 1M
(and users could bump this value if needed), and far below the true system
max. Kernel memory usage is ~300MB currently, increasing that by around
90MB is ~30% increase.

Regarding IOPS, metadata is much lower volume than data.

Thanks,
Tom & Henry

