Hi, Tom,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1. "In the case that B3 was elected, with the higher end offset of offset
7. It will perform replica reconciliation and discard its data that is
greater than the high watermark of 5. This prevents any sacrifices in
availability, and is equivalent to standard Kafka."

Hmm, this is not how the current replication works. If B3 becomes the
leader, it won't truncate any of its data. So B2 will need to get the data
for offset 6 from S3E1Z in AZ1, which is unavailable. Are you changing the
current replication protocol? Also, have you solved the availability
problem during reassignment?

JR2. "Are you referring to data or metadata?"

I am referring to the data.

"For data, there is a combiner thread on the leader, which combines data
from multiple partitions into one large S3 object. This same object is
accessed by all followers of those partitions. However, they only access a
specific byte range for the partitions they are following. Thus, it doesn't
matter if they are accessing the same object or separate object. They need
to pay the request cost and data transfer either way."

Hmm, we went through the cost calculation earlier and agreed that the
number of read requests matters, right?

"write cost =3D 1 put request cost + transfer cost
($0.00113) / (1000 PUT) + ($0.0032 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 1.13 * 10^-6 + 0.96 =
*
10^-6 =3D $2.09 * 10^-6

read cost =3D 100 read requests cost + transfer cost
100 * ($0.00003) / (1000 GET) + ($0.0006 / 1 GB) * 0.3MB =3D 3 * 10^-6 + 0.=
18
* 10^-6 =3D $3.18 * 10^-6

As you can see, the read cost is actually much higher than the write cost.
Is that reflected in your calculation?"


JR8.
"Correct, for each data partition, there is a 1-1 mapping to the metadata
partition. From all solutions we evaluated, this is the only one where we
can both 1. reduce metadata read fanout and 2. avoid significant
architecture change to add a centralized coordinator (e.g., receives all
metadata and writes to a performant datastore, then as followers request
metadata it performs key-value lookups for offsets)"

Have you looked at the design in
https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAFKA/KIP-1164%3A+Topic+Based+B=
atch+Coordinator?
The coordinator is backed by an internal topic with a fixed number of
partitions. Each broker retrieves only the relevant metadata from the
coordinator through an API.

"So total file descriptors would remain below the initial lower bound of 1M
(and users could bump this value if needed), and far below the true system
max. Kernel memory usage is ~300MB currently, increasing that by around
90MB is ~30% increase.

Regarding IOPS, metadata is much lower volume than data."

My main point is that partitions are not free, so we should be careful
about adding too many. For example, durability is achieved by flushing the
data/metadata to disk. Flushing each partition requires a separate IO. If
the disk has 1K IOPS and we have 5K partitions in a broker, we can afford
to flush each partition every 5 seconds, achieving a RPO of 5 seconds. If
we double the number of partitions, we can only flush each partition every
10 seconds, which makes RPO twice as bad. As another example, too many
partitions tend to slow down replication, adding more latency for clients.

Jun

