 Ivan,
For the answer on how many data/meta-data partitions the diskless KIP needs=
 to create (hence the number of open file descriptors), there seems to be a=
 missing gap.
In the current Kafka design, for each topic partition, there is a correspon=
ding log directory and a sequence of log segment files on the broker disk f=
or persistence. =C2=A0For example, /mnt/kafka/partitions/topic0-0/00000.log=
 for topic 0 partition 0 (I used /mnt/kafka/partitions as the root log dire=
ctory name for illustration purpose since that is the directory name in our=
 system). =C2=A0After user enabled topic0 to be diskless, the content in th=
e log data file now contains the pointers to WAL log segment file in S3 (th=
e metadata) so you are re-using the file descriptors in /mnt/kafka/partitio=
ns, but what about the real data content for topic0-0? =C2=A0I think the KI=
P is proposing uploading those data as part of the WAL log segment onto S3,=
 but there also needs to be a local cached copy of data content for topic0-=
0 on both the leader broker as well as the follower brokers for consumers t=
o read from.
The KIP did not mention how that cache is built or organized or whether it =
is disk backed. =C2=A0I remember in the earlier replies you mentioned you a=
re going to reuse Kafka's existing page cache system for the data content c=
ache, but if you are reusing Kafka's page cache, the data you downloaded fr=
om S3 or received from produceRequest for topic0-0 will needs to be stored =
in /mnt/kafka/partitions/topic0-0 (or maybe a new directory such as /mnt/ka=
fka/partitions/data-topic0-0 to avoid the name conflict), but if you do thi=
s mapping for 100 original data partitions you will end up with 100 more ne=
w partition directories and file descriptors on the disk.
Also for the followers to build that cache, there will be temporary file an=
d file descriptors being created for the S3 download, and those will be new=
 file descriptors being created constantly for each data segment you are do=
wnloading from S3.
    On Tuesday, October 14, 2025 at 11:03:46 AM PDT, Ivan Yurchenko <ivan@i=
vanyu.me> wrote: =20
=20
 Hi Jun,

Thank you for your message. I=E2=80=99m sorry that I failed to clearly expl=
ain the idea. Let me try to fix this.

> Does each partition now have a metadata partition and a separate data
> partition? If so, I am concerned that it essentially doubles the number o=
f
> partitions, which impacts the number of open file descriptors and the
> required IOPS, and so on. It also seems wasteful to have a separate
> partition just to store the metadata. It's as if we are creating an
> internal topic with an unbounded number of partitions.

No. There will be only one physical partition per diskless partition. Let m=
e explain this with an example. Let=E2=80=99s say we have a diskless partit=
ion topic-0. It has three replicas 0, 1, 2; 0 is the leader. We produce som=
e batches to this partition. The content of the segment file will be someth=
ing like this (for each batch):

BaseOffset: 00000000000000000000 (like in classic)
Length: 123456 (like in classic)
PartitionLeaderEpoch: like in classic
Magic: like in classic
CRC: like in classic
Attributes: like in classic
LastOffsetDelta: like in classic
BaseTimestamp: like in classic
MaxTimestamp: like in classic
ProducerId: like in classic
ProducerEpoch: like in classic
BaseSequence: like in classic
RecordsCount: like in classic
Records:
=C2=A0 path/to/wal/files/5b55c4bb-f52a-4204-aea6-81226895158a; byte offset =
123456

It looks very much like classic log entries. The only difference is that in=
stead of writing real Records, we write the reference to the WAL file with =
the batch data (I guess we need only the name and the byte offset, because =
the byte length is the standard field above). Otherwise, it=E2=80=99s a nor=
mal Kafka log with the leader and replicas.=20

So we have as many partitions for diskless as for classic. As of open file =
descriptors, let=E2=80=99s proceed to the following:

> Are the metadata and
> the data for the same partition always collocated on the same broker? If
> so, how do we enforce that when replicas are reassigned?

The source of truth for the data is still in WAL files on object storage. T=
he source of truth for the metadata is in segment files on the brokers in t=
he replica set. Two new mechanisms are planned, both independent of this ne=
w proposal, but I want to present them to give the idea that only a limited=
 amount of data files will be operated locally:
- We want to assemble batches into segment files and offload them to tiered=
 storage in order to prevent the unbounded growth of batch metadata. For th=
is, we need to open only=C2=A0 a few file descriptors (for the segment file=
 itself + the necessary indexes) before the segment is fully written and ha=
nded over to RemoteLogManager.
- We want to assemble local segment files for caching purposes as well, i.e=
. to speed up fetching. This will not materialize the full content of the l=
og, but only the hot set according to some policy (or configurable policies=
), i.e. the number of segments and file descriptors will also be limited.

> The number of RPCs in the produce path is significantly higher. For
> example, if a produce request has 100 partitions, in a cluster with 100
> brokers, each produce request could generate 100 more RPC requests. This
> will significantly increase the request rate.

This is a valid concern that we considered, but this issue can be mitigated=
. I=E2=80=99ll try to explain the approach.
The situation with a single broker is trivial: all the commit requests go f=
rom the broker to itself.=20
Let=E2=80=99s scale this to a multi-broker cluster, but located in the sing=
le rack (AZ). Any broker can accept Produce requests for diskless partition=
s, but we can tell producers (through metadata) to always send Produce requ=
ests to leaders. For example, broker 0 hosts the leader replicas for diskle=
ss partitions t1-0, t2-1, t3-0. It will receive diskless Produce requests f=
or these partitions in various combinations, but only for them.

