Hi Tom,

For JR1,
I agree with Jun that in the current replication protocol, we always treat
the leader as the source of truth.
That means, the log truncation will always happen on the follower side, not
the leader.

For the leader AZ down issue, have you considered a simpler solution to
fallback to the classic replication protocol?

In this example, suppose there is a remote WAL enabled topic also with
replication factor 3 (for a fair comparison). The current leader of a
partition is B1. B1 has received data up to offset 10, written data to
S3E1Z up to offset 10, and published metadata up to offset 10. One
follower, B2, has replicated up to offset 5, and follower B3, up to offset
7. Suppose B1 dies (e.g., AZ goes down):

a. B3 is elected as the new leader, B2 is trying to fetch offset 6 from the
inaccessible S3E1Z, then fallback to normal replication protocol to fetch
from the new leader B3. Until it tries to fetch data from offset 11, it
will get back to fetch from S3E1Z in B3 AZ. (Of course we can try to delete
the metadata after LEO offset 7 like you proposed earlier, but since the
new leader _should_ be in the ISR if not unclean leader, the offset gap
should be small, the overhead should be able to be ignored.)
b. B2 is elected as the new leader, B3 should truncate to offset 5, and B2
continues with normal S3E1Z upload in KIP-1176 when receiving producer
writes, and when fetching from offset 6~10, it is fetching via classic
replication protocol.

I think this solution makes it cleaner and simpler. WDYT?


Thanks
Luke

