Hi Henry,

Indeed, there will be some additional files and descriptors, but as I me=
ntioned in my previous message, we don't think there will be a lot more =
compared to the classic Kafka partitions as baseline. We will not cache =
every batch or other small amount in a separate file on disk. There will=
 normal Kafka segments (likely in a separate directory as you said) to a=
ppend these batches sequentially. Only recent data will be cached this w=
ay, so this cache will not grow (neither in disk usage nor in the number=
 of file descriptors) proportionally to the total partition size.=20

You're right that this caching/log materialization part is indeed a litt=
le underspecified at the moment. We're sorry for this, but as an excuse,=
 we're trying to fix the most fundamental parts of the design first, suc=
h as the approach to the batch coordinator. Local log materialization is=
 mostly independent of the coordinator design and may be discussed down =
the road.

Best,
Ivan

On Thu, Oct 16, 2025, at 01:42, Henry Haiying Cai wrote:
> Ivan,
> For the answer on how many data/meta-data partitions the diskless KIP =
needs to create (hence the number of open file descriptors), there seems=
 to be a missing gap.
> In the current Kafka design, for each topic partition, there is a corr=
esponding log directory and a sequence of log segment files on the broke=
r disk for persistence.  For example, /mnt/kafka/partitions/topic0-0/000=
00.log for topic 0 partition 0 (I used /mnt/kafka/partitions as the root=
 log directory name for illustration purpose since that is the directory=
 name in our system).  After user enabled topic0 to be diskless, the con=
tent in the log data file now contains the pointers to WAL log segment f=
ile in S3 (the metadata) so you are re-using the file descriptors in /mn=
t/kafka/partitions, but what about the real data content for topic0-0?  =
I think the KIP is proposing uploading those data as part of the WAL log=
 segment onto S3, but there also needs to be a local cached copy of data=
 content for topic0-0 on both the leader broker as well as the follower =
brokers for consumers to read from.
> The KIP did not mention how that cache is built or organized or whethe=
r it is disk backed.  I remember in the earlier replies you mentioned yo=
u are going to reuse Kafka's existing page cache system for the data con=
tent cache, but if you are reusing Kafka's page cache, the data you down=
loaded from S3 or received from produceRequest for topic0-0 will needs t=
o be stored in /mnt/kafka/partitions/topic0-0 (or maybe a new directory =
such as /mnt/kafka/partitions/data-topic0-0 to avoid the name conflict),=
 but if you do this mapping for 100 original data partitions you will en=
d up with 100 more new partition directories and file descriptors on the=
 disk.
> Also for the followers to build that cache, there will be temporary fi=
le and file descriptors being created for the S3 download, and those wil=
l be new file descriptors being created constantly for each data segment=
 you are downloading from S3.
>     On Tuesday, October 14, 2025 at 11:03:46 AM PDT, Ivan Yurchenko <i=
van@ivanyu.me> wrote: =20
> =20
> Hi Jun,
>=20
> Thank you for your message. I=E2=80=99m sorry that I failed to clearly=
 explain the idea. Let me try to fix this.
>=20
> > Does each partition now have a metadata partition and a separate data
> > partition? If so, I am concerned that it essentially doubles the num=
ber of
> > partitions, which impacts the number of open file descriptors and the
> > required IOPS, and so on. It also seems wasteful to have a separate
> > partition just to store the metadata. It's as if we are creating an
> > internal topic with an unbounded number of partitions.
>=20
> No. There will be only one physical partition per diskless partition. =
Let me explain this with an example. Let=E2=80=99s say we have a diskles=
s partition topic-0. It has three replicas 0, 1, 2; 0 is the leader. We =
produce some batches to this partition. The content of the segment file =
will be something like this (for each batch):
>=20
> BaseOffset: 00000000000000000000 (like in classic)
> Length: 123456 (like in classic)
> PartitionLeaderEpoch: like in classic
> Magic: like in classic
> CRC: like in classic
> Attributes: like in classic
> LastOffsetDelta: like in classic
> BaseTimestamp: like in classic
> MaxTimestamp: like in classic
> ProducerId: like in classic
> ProducerEpoch: like in classic
> BaseSequence: like in classic
> RecordsCount: like in classic
> Records:
>   path/to/wal/files/5b55c4bb-f52a-4204-aea6-81226895158a; byte offset =
123456
>=20
> It looks very much like classic log entries. The only difference is th=
at instead of writing real Records, we write the reference to the WAL fi=
le with the batch data (I guess we need only the name and the byte offse=
t, because the byte length is the standard field above). Otherwise, it=E2=
=80=99s a normal Kafka log with the leader and replicas.=20
>=20
> So we have as many partitions for diskless as for classic. As of open =
file descriptors, let=E2=80=99s proceed to the following:
>=20
> > Are the metadata and
> > the data for the same partition always collocated on the same broker=
? If
> > so, how do we enforce that when replicas are reassigned?
>=20
> The source of truth for the data is still in WAL files on object stora=
ge. The source of truth for the metadata is in segment files on the brok=
ers in the replica set. Two new mechanisms are planned, both independent=
 of this new proposal, but I want to present them to give the idea that =
only a limited amount of data files will be operated locally:
> - We want to assemble batches into segment files and offload them to t=
iered storage in order to prevent the unbounded growth of batch metadata=
. For this, we need to open only  a few file descriptors (for the segmen=
t file itself + the necessary indexes) before the segment is fully writt=
en and handed over to RemoteLogManager.
> - We want to assemble local segment files for caching purposes as well=
, i.e. to speed up fetching. This will not materialize the full content =
of the log, but only the hot set according to some policy (or configurab=
le policies), i.e. the number of segments and file descriptors will also=
 be limited.
>=20
> > The number of RPCs in the produce path is significantly higher. For
> > example, if a produce request has 100 partitions, in a cluster with =
100
> > brokers, each produce request could generate 100 more RPC requests. =
This
> > will significantly increase the request rate.
>=20
> This is a valid concern that we considered, but this issue can be miti=
gated. I=E2=80=99ll try to explain the approach.
> The situation with a single broker is trivial: all the commit requests=
 go from the broker to itself.=20
> Let=E2=80=99s scale this to a multi-broker cluster, but located in the=
 single rack (AZ). Any broker can accept Produce requests for diskless p=
artitions, but we can tell producers (through metadata) to always send P=
roduce requests to leaders. For example, broker 0 hosts the leader repli=
cas for diskless partitions t1-0, t2-1, t3-0. It will receive diskless P=
roduce requests for these partitions in various combinations, but only f=
or them.
>=20
>                     Broker 0
>               +-----------------+
>               |    t1-0        |
>               |    t2-1 <--------------------+
>               |    t3-0        |            |
> produce      | +-------------+ |            |
> requests    | |  diskless  | |            |
> --------------->|  produce  +--------------+
> for these    | | WAL buffer  | |    commit requests
> partitions  | +-------------+ |    for these partitions
>               |                |
>               +-----------------+
>=20
> The same applies for other brokers in this cluster. Effectively, each =
broker will commit only to itself, which effectively means 1 commit requ=
est per WAL buffer (this may be 0 physical network calls, if we wish, ju=
st a local function call).
>=20
> Now let=E2=80=99s scale this to multiple racks (AZs). Obviously, we ca=
nnot always send Produce requests to the designated leaders of diskless =
partitions: this would mean inter-AZ network traffic, which we would lik=
e to avoid. To avoid it, we say that every broker has a =E2=80=9Cdiskles=
s produce representative=E2=80=9D in every AZ. If we continue our exampl=
e: when a Produce request for t1-0, t2-1, or t3-0 comes from a producer =
in AZ 0, it lands on broker 0 (in the broker=E2=80=99s AZ the representa=
tive is the broker itself). However, if it comes from AZ 1, it lands on =
broker 1; in AZ 2, it=E2=80=99s broker 2.=20
>=20
>   |produce requests        |produce requests        |produce requests
>   |for t1-0, t2-1, t3-0    |for t1-0, t2-1, t3-0    |for t1-0, t2-1, t=
3-0
>   |from AZ 0                |from AZ 1              |from AZ 2
>   v                        v                        v
> Broker 0 (AZ 0)        Broker 1 (AZ 1)        Broker 2 (AZ 2)
> +---------------+      +---------------+      +---------------+
> |    t1-0      |      |              |      |              |
> |    t2-1      |      |              |      |              |
> |    t3-0      |      |              |      |              |
> +---------------+      +--------+------+      +--------+------+
>     ^    ^                    |                      |
>     |    +--------------------+                      |
>     |    commit requests for these partitions        |
>     |                                                |
>     +-------------------------------------------------+
>           commit requests for these partitions
>=20
> All the partitions that broker 0 is the leader of will be =E2=80=9Crep=
resented=E2=80=9D by brokers 1 and 2 in their AZs.
>=20
> Of course, this relationship goes both ways between AZs (not necessari=
ly between the same brokers). It means that provided the cluster is bala=
nced by the number of brokers per AZ, each broker will represent (number=
_of_azs - 1) other brokers. This will result in the situation that for t=
he majority of commits, each broker will do up to (number_of_azs - 1) ne=
twork commit requests (plus one local). Cloud regions tend to have 3 AZs=
, very rarely more. That means, brokers will be doing up to 2 network co=
mmit requests per WAL file.
>=20
> There are the following exceptions:
> 1. Broker count imbalance between AZs. For example, when we have 2 AZs=
 and one has three brokers and another AZ has one. This one broker will =
do between 1 and 3 commit requests per WAL file. This is not an extreme =
amplification. Such an imbalance is not healthy in most practical setups=
 and should be avoided anyway.
> 2. Leadership changes and metadata propagation period. When the partit=
ion t3-0 is relocated from broker 0 to some broker 3, the producers will=
 not know this immediately (unless we want to be strict and respond with=
 NOT_LEADER_OR_FOLLOWER). So if t1-0, t2-1, and t3-0 will come together =
in a WAL buffer on broker 2, it will have to send two commit requests: t=
o broker 0 to commit t1-0 and t2-1, and to broker 3 to commit t3-0. This=
 situation is not permanent and as producers update the cluster metadata=
, it will be resolved.
>=20
> This all could be built with the metadata crafting mechanism only (whi=
ch is anyway needed for Diskless in one way or another to direct produce=
rs and consumers where we need to avoid inter-AZ traffic), just with the=
 right policy for it (for example, some deterministic hash-based formula=
). I.e. no explicit support for =E2=80=9Cproduce representative=E2=80=9D=
 or anything like this is needed on the cluster level, in KRaft, etc.
>=20
> > The same WAL file metadata is now duplicated into two places, partit=
ion
> > leader and WAL File Manager. Which one is the source of truth, and h=
ow do
> > we maintain consistency between the two places?
