Hi all,

I feel we are at a kind of fork in the road at this point as to the
direction we want to take with this KIP. I think it's worth taking a step
back for a moment to evaluate what we really want for Apache Kafka in terms
of properties such as elasticity, operational complexity balanced by cost
of implementation and ongoing code maintenance.

We have three major revisions:
 rev1. The original version of the KIP. Stateless producer and consumer
serving, Batch Coordinators for sequencing and batch coordinate storage.
 rev2. Use tiered storage for long term data management, with local log
segment caching entering the write-path to long term storage. Producer load
still leaderless but consumption side is more stateful/sticky and
requiring disks.
 rev3. No more batch coordinators. Replace with topic partitions to do per
TP sequencing/metadata storage (enables code reuse for txns) and a WAL File
Manager to handle WAL cleanup. Plus a few new non-trivial hurdles to
overcome.

I would propose to the community that we evaluate two broad approaches,
both of which more of less map onto KIP-1150 (in one of its forms):
A) Do we want a more cloud native design, with better separation between
stateless and stateful components, but more costly in engineering effort
and maintenance (due to duplication of txns and stuff like that). The north
star being stateless and diskless producer/consumer serving nodes (like the
'proxy' role in WarpStream), then batch coordinators acting as the stateful
coordination component.
B. Or do we want to prioritize reduced long-term code maintenance and
upfront implementation effort, by reusing existing code for txns, using
tiered storage for long term data management (instead of S3 topic specific
object compaction), by sacrificing on elasticity, placing disks in the
(async) write-path for S3 topics.

Preferring (A) means a design more similar to the first version of this
KIP. If we prefer (B) then that aligns more with revisions 2/3.

I'd like to hear from other community members on what kind of solution they
think best equips Kafka for the next decade.

Thanks
Jack Vanlightly

