Hi Jun,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1. "Hmm, this is not how the current replication works. If B3 becomes the
leader, it won't truncate any of its data. So B2 will need to get the data
for offset 6 from S3E1Z in AZ1, which is unavailable. Are you changing the
current replication protocol? Also, have you solved the availability
problem during reassignment?"

For the case of B2 elected, it is covered by existing replica resolution
(follower truncates its logs). For the case of B3 elected, we propose
either of the following solutions:
1. New Leader Uploads in Reverse Order: When the new leader B3 is selected,
it uploads its logs in reverse order to its AZ, ie AZ2. That way the
follower, B2, can immediately get the latest records. This means the leader
becomes available much sooner, and followers can receive data sooner. All
data from B3 will be persisted in AZ2 eventually, but the new leader will
not need to block on uploading that old historical data that B2 already has=
.
2. Standard Replication During New Leader Election (Luke's Suggestion):
After B3 is elected, it will serve a standard fetch request for its
followers and send data as if in normal replication. Once the follower has
read up to the new leader LEO, then it will switch to replication via
S3E1Z. As long as it was a clean leader election, the amount of data sent
to catch up the follower(s) should be small.

Do you have any preference between these two proposals?

JR2. "Hmm, we went through the cost calculation earlier and agreed that the
number of read requests matters, right?"

Yes, read requests do matter. The key part is the ratio of write to read
requests. If a single write produces hundreds of read requests, then that
read cost becomes large (possibly greater than the write cost). Since write
cost is ~37x what read cost is, there is an approximate tipping point
reached then. Given that the broker may be leading hundreds of remote wal
enabled partitions, we propose another possible solution:

Intra-AZ Data Replicator Nodes: The core idea is that when a write object
is performed, we nominate two specific follower brokers, one in each
follower AZ, as data replicators for all followers also in that AZ. These
will perform one object read, and then provide the necessary data to all
followers within that AZ.

We will walk through an example, using familiar values from before. B1 (in
AZ1) is the leader of 100 partitions. It receives 300KB of data across
those partitions. It writes the 300KB object to storage. It produces a
metadata event for the entire object, which includes the AZ replicator
nodes, randomly selected as B2 (in AZ2) & B3 (in AZ3). The individual
metadata events for each partition/byte range are also produced. All
follower brokers in the range of B2.1 to B2.N make read requests from B2.
B2 only performs a single GET request for the object & caches the result.
It can then serve each read request for desired byte ranges for specific
partitions. B3 does the equivalent operation. This allows us to reduce our
read operations from # Followers * # Partitions Per Object, which in the
original case was 2 * 100 =3D 200 GETs. Now the number of GETs is simply #
Followers. By randomly nominating each AZ replicator we can ensure the load
is evenly distributed. Since the AZ-replicator is in the same AZ of the
requests, the data transfer is free.

What do you think of this approach? Do you see a more elegant way to avoid
the repeated read problem?

JR8.
"Have you looked at the design in
https://urldefense.com/v3/__https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/KAF=
KA/KIP-1164*3A*Topic*Based*Batch*Coordinator?__;JSsrKys!!DCbAVzZNrAf4!CLwdj=
TAEqo8fG3plu4p8DtVeBY5GqZxjrB7ugwUc1QelLtszvsbv0h1NA8P8yOLrjAE7nLvokzOS6rmf=
P6YMM7N7$
The coordinator is backed by an internal topic with a fixed number of
partitions. Each broker retrieves only the relevant metadata from the
coordinator through an API."

Yes we have reviewed that design. I forgot to add one other requirement we
had which is that we wanted a solution that did not expand the dependencies
needed by Kafka. The KIP-1164 solution relies on SQLite in order to be
scalable (i.e. the state becomes too large for memory). Our solution of
paired topics does not have any additional dependency. Another reason we
prefer ours is that there is clear life-cycle management and isolation in
case of failures. As topics are created/deleted with remote WAL enabled, we
can create/delete the corresponding metadata topics cleanly. Versus with
KIP-1150 the topic has infinite retention and can't easily clean up the
metadata for deleted or disabled topics. Additionally, in the case of an
offline partition, all data topics that map to that partition are affected,
so it can be a single point of failure for multiple topics. Versus having
an isolated metadata topic for each partition, if that metadata partition
is offline, it only affects that specific topic-partition.

"My main point is that partitions are not free, so we should be careful
about adding too many. For example, durability is achieved by flushing the
data/metadata to disk. Flushing each partition requires a separate IO. If
the disk has 1K IOPS and we have 5K partitions in a broker, we can afford
to flush each partition every 5 seconds, achieving a RPO of 5 seconds. If
we double the number of partitions, we can only flush each partition every
10 seconds, which makes RPO twice as bad. As another example, too many
partitions tend to slow down replication, adding more latency for clients."

In the case of the remote WAL enabled topic, the metadata topic's file
segments will need to be flushed as those are replicated as normal between
brokers. However, for the data file segment topics, we can rely on cloud
storage for durability. If we stop flushing the data to disk, then we can
remove that IOPS requirement. Thus the net result is the same IOPS.

Thanks,
Tom

