Hi Luke,

Thanks for your reply and the idea for how to solve leader failover. I've
included it in my reply to Jun as one of the proposed solutions. I've
copied an excerpt here:

"For the case of B2 elected, it is covered by existing replica resolution
(follower truncates its logs). For the case of B3 elected, we propose
either of the following solutions:
1. New Leader Uploads in Reverse Order: When the new leader B3 is selected,
it uploads its logs in reverse order to its AZ, ie AZ2. That way the
follower, B2, can immediately get the latest records. This means the leader
becomes available much sooner, and followers can receive data sooner. All
data from B3 will be persisted in AZ2 eventually, but the new leader will
not need to block on uploading that old historical data that B2 already has=
.
2. Standard Replication During New Leader Election (Luke's Suggestion):
After B3 is elected, it will serve a standard fetch request for its
followers and send data as if in normal replication. Once the follower has
read up to the new leader LEO, then it will switch to replication via
S3E1Z. As long as it was a clean leader election, the amount of data sent
to catch up the follower(s) should be small."

Do you still prefer your approach? The data cost is marginal to catch up
with the follower normally, so it is primarily about how clean the code
would be. If data was large, solution #1 would be more cost efficient. For
solution 2, there will be some state needed to track when to switch between
normal replication and cloud storage replication. For solution 1, there may
be two threads, one is uploading data in reverse order during new leader
election, the other thread is uploading data as it is received. Once the
first thread completes the historical upload, cloud based replication will
continue as normal.

Thanks,
Tom

