Hi Tom,

Uploading in Reverse Order is also a good idea, but might take longer to
get recovered than fallback to standard classic replication protocol.
Regarding the costs, since we are comparing a small number of PUT VS
cross-AZ traffic, it should have no much difference in my opinion.

About JR8: for each data partition, there is a 1-1 mapping to the metadata
partition:
I also don't think having 1 data partition + 1 metadata partition for all
partitions is a good idea as we are increasing the resource usage now, not
just the storage, but also the CPU/Memory. If you accept to have another
metadata partition to store the metadata, have you reconsidered my previous
suggestion about relying on fetchResponse to carry the metadata to
followers? The main rationale is because when the followers successfully
download the logs from the fast cloud storage, the metadata is useless
anymore. So we can pass these metadata via fetchResponse to followers. More
details can check this comment:
https://lists.apache.org/thread/v8t7co0517hw2tlm0ypn8tnjfmhnhv83

Thank you.
Luke



On Sat, Oct 18, 2025 at 7:28=E2=80=AFAM Thomas Thornton
<tthornton@salesforce.com.invalid> wrote:

> Hi Luke,
>
> Thanks for your reply and the idea for how to solve leader failover. I've
> included it in my reply to Jun as one of the proposed solutions. I've
> copied an excerpt here:
>
> "For the case of B2 elected, it is covered by existing replica resolution
> (follower truncates its logs). For the case of B3 elected, we propose
> either of the following solutions:
> 1. New Leader Uploads in Reverse Order: When the new leader B3 is selecte=
d,
> it uploads its logs in reverse order to its AZ, ie AZ2. That way the
> follower, B2, can immediately get the latest records. This means the lead=
er
> becomes available much sooner, and followers can receive data sooner. All
> data from B3 will be persisted in AZ2 eventually, but the new leader will
> not need to block on uploading that old historical data that B2 already
> has.
> 2. Standard Replication During New Leader Election (Luke's Suggestion):
> After B3 is elected, it will serve a standard fetch request for its
> followers and send data as if in normal replication. Once the follower ha=
s
> read up to the new leader LEO, then it will switch to replication via
> S3E1Z. As long as it was a clean leader election, the amount of data sent
