Hi, Tom,

Thanks for the reply.

JR1.

Approach 1 increases latency when the leader changes and complicates
reading the metadata because the ordering is not guaranteed. Neither
approach addresses the availability issue when a partition is reassigned to
a new replica and an S3EZ1 is down.

My bigger concern is that this type of change is quite intrusive to the
classic topic's code path, but it's only needed for this particular design
of leveraging S3EZ1. If we use S3, we likely won't need any of those
changes to handle unavailability. Ideally, we want to use the same
framework to cover both S3 and S3EZ1.

JR2.

Yes, the concept of this approach works. But we need to do this in a clean
way that works with things like partition reassignment.

JR8.

"The KIP-1164 solution relies on SQLite in order to be
scalable (i.e. the state becomes too large for memory). Our solution of
paired topics does not have any additional dependency."

If we compact objects periodically, say every few minutes, the metadata
could potentially fit in memory. Then we don't necessarily need the SQLite
dependency.

"Versus with KIP-1150 the topic has infinite retention and can't easily
clean up the metadata for deleted or disabled topics."

This is a potential problem, but we can see how easy it is to solve it.

"Additionally, in the case of an offline partition, all data topics that
map to that partition are affected,"

This is not a strong argument since each partition is highly available with
multiple replicas.

"However, for the data file segment topics, we can rely on cloud
storage for durability. If we stop flushing the data to disk, then we can
remove that IOPS requirement. Thus the net result is the same IOPS."

If we have a mix of classic topics and WAL enaled topics on the same
broker, it's important that the classic topics' data is flushed to disk as
quickly as possible. To achieve this, users typically set
dirty_expire_centisecs in the kernel based on the number of disk IOPS
available. Once you set this number, it applies to all dirty files,
including the cached data in WAL enabled topics. So, if there are more
files actively accumulating data, the flush frequency and therefore RPO is
reduced.

Thanks,

Jun

